1️⃣1️⃣ NAT

NAT = Network Address Translation

It translates addresses between networks, commonly:

Private IP
    ↓
NAT Router
    ↓
Public IP
    ↓
Internet

Example:

192.168.1.10
      ↓
    NAT
      ↓
203.x.x.x


---

1️⃣2️⃣ Why NAT?

Private networks can use private IPv4 addresses internally.

NAT allows many internal devices to share one or more public addresses.

Example:

Laptop ─────┐
Phone ──────┤
Tablet ─────┼──→ NAT Router → Internet
TV ─────────┘


---

1️⃣3️⃣ PAT

PAT = Port Address Translation

Also commonly called NAT overload.

It allows many internal connections to share one public IP by distinguishing connections using port numbers.

Example:

192.168.1.10:5000
        ↓
203.x.x.x:40001

192.168.1.11:5001
        ↓
203.x.x.x:40002


---

🔥 1️⃣4️⃣ NAT vs PAT

NAT	PAT

Translates IP addresses	Translates IP + uses ports
Can map addresses in different ways	Commonly many-to-one public IP sharing
Broader concept	Specific/common NAT technique



